1
00:00:01,620 --> 00:00:05,360
To talk about my childhood experiences in Trung Liet area...

2
00:00:06,160 --> 00:00:07,850
to be honest, I don't have too many.

3
00:00:08,980 --> 00:00:11,440
Well... because the thing about the Trung Liet area is,

4
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:17,160
my family didn't live there; we just had a store there. So even though I

5
00:00:17,160 --> 00:00:18,300
often went there to play

6
00:00:19,020 --> 00:00:21,800
with my mother and the store clerks,

7
00:00:22,660 --> 00:00:25,900
it wasn't the place where I lived. So I didn't play there

8
00:00:25,900 --> 00:00:26,340
that much.

9
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:31,940
But since it was on my mother's way to work, she enrolled me in extra classes

10
00:00:31,940 --> 00:00:35,840
nearby. So I would attend the classes and then head to the store to play.

11
00:00:36,480 --> 00:00:39,280
These extra classes only went on during

12
00:00:39,900 --> 00:00:40,600
my elementary school years.

13
00:01:01,550 --> 00:01:02,300
So, um...

20
00:01:03,100 --> 00:01:05,980
I still remember in the afternoons, after we finished lunch,

21
00:01:06,660 --> 00:01:11,590
we would rest for a bit. Then around 4 or 5 PM, my mom would start

22
00:01:11,590 --> 00:01:12,920
walking me to the class.

23
00:01:13,860 --> 00:01:17,400
Back then, I remember all I could see was—

24
00:01:19,540 --> 00:01:24,900
first of all, there weren't many cars.

25
00:01:25,720 --> 00:01:27,040
The other day, on my current commute back home from work,

26
00:01:27,800 --> 00:01:31,460
I passed by that prep classroom again, and it caught me off guard.

27
00:01:31,460 --> 00:01:35,620
Because that alley—the last time I saw it as a child,

28
00:01:35,620 --> 00:01:37,480
I had never seen a car parked there.

29
00:01:38,420 --> 00:01:41,940
But when I passed by the other day, I saw a car parked right in the alley.

30
00:01:41,940 --> 00:01:44,600
It was a rather strange feeling. Of course, seeing cars in Hanoi

31
00:01:45,260 --> 00:01:50,300
is a very normal thing for me now,

32
00:01:51,160 --> 00:01:55,800
but realizing that after more than ten years of not visiting,

33
00:01:55,800 --> 00:01:57,340
the motorbikes there have been replaced by cars—I found that quite fascinating.

34
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:03,660
It shows that Hanoi has changed so much.

35
00:02:03,660 --> 00:02:05,140
Even though motorbikes are still extremely common in Vietnam,

36
00:02:05,800 --> 00:02:09,960
cars have also become very popular.

37
00:02:09,960 --> 00:02:10,460
Along with that, the sounds on the street are very different now.

38
00:02:12,320 --> 00:02:15,240
The street sounds back then in Trung Liet were very quiet because it was a small street.

39
00:02:15,920 --> 00:02:21,870
Back then, the street named Nguyen Van Tuyet hadn't been built yet.

40
00:02:21,870 --> 00:02:26,020
Where Nguyen Van Tuyet street is now, it used to be a ditch.

41
00:02:26,020 --> 00:02:31,340
Later on, they paved it over and built the road.

42
00:02:31,340 --> 00:02:34,720
Since it was just a ditch back then, no vehicles drove through there.

43
00:02:34,720 --> 00:02:35,700
Our street was very quiet.

44
00:02:36,340 --> 00:02:38,020
But now that the road is open, the street is extremely crowded.

45
00:03:00,600 --> 00:03:04,440
In fact, Nguyen Van Tuyet Street has been turned into a food street.

46
00:03:05,080 --> 00:03:09,860
So there is a constant flow of traffic,

47
00:03:11,360 --> 00:03:16,540
and it's always very busy and noisy. At night, you can even hear music thumping from the bars.

48
00:03:19,360 --> 00:03:24,740
That's what I experience when I pass by that street in 2026.

49
00:03:24,740 --> 00:03:28,100
However, my childhood was spent on a very peaceful street,

50
00:03:30,340 --> 00:03:33,700
with barely any traffic—mostly just neighbors who all knew each other.

51
00:03:34,520 --> 00:03:39,940
I still remember on my way to prep class back in first grade,

52
00:03:39,940 --> 00:03:45,280
I would always walk past an apartment complex.

53
00:03:45,280 --> 00:03:46,680
Beneath that building, there was a children's playground.

54
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:52,380
To this day, I have never set foot inside that playground to play,

55
00:03:52,620 --> 00:03:54,500
even though I've walked past it countless times.

56
00:03:55,300 --> 00:04:00,240
Because [laughs] I was always there just to go to the store or to class,

57
00:04:00,920 --> 00:04:04,500
rather than to play. So I never got to go inside.

58
00:04:04,500 --> 00:04:10,300
But I still clearly remember the feeling: as a five-year-old child walking to class,

59
00:04:10,300 --> 00:04:14,660
seeing other kids playing so happily there.

60
00:04:14,660 --> 00:04:15,040
It was a feeling of intense longing and anticipation—

61
00:04:15,700 --> 00:04:16,700
wishing I could stay and play, or throw a tantrum just so I could join them.

62
00:04:18,019 --> 00:04:20,120
That feeling has stayed with me since childhood.

63
00:04:20,820 --> 00:04:20,990
I think

64
00:04:21,940 --> 00:04:25,940
if this street disappears, that feeling will fade away with it.

65
00:04:25,940 --> 00:04:29,500
Whenever I walk past that street now and see the playground,

66
00:04:29,500 --> 00:04:34,640
I can still recall the exact emotion of being a five-year-old who had to go to class

67
00:04:34,640 --> 00:04:40,120
while other kids were playing.

68
00:04:40,120 --> 00:04:43,560
It's a feeling that only I can experience, and one that is hard to describe in words.

69
00:04:44,340 --> 00:04:50,640
I wasn't jealous of them; it was just the simple desire of a child who wanted to play but was forced to study.

70
00:04:50,640 --> 00:04:53,890
It's an experience I will never have again because I've grown up.

71
00:04:53,890 --> 00:04:57,940
But if this playground is cleared and this whole street where our store is gets demolished,

72
00:04:57,940 --> 00:05:01,969
I will never again be able to walk past it as an adult and recall those childhood feelings.

73
00:05:01,969 --> 00:05:05,750
So this demolition erases a part of my emotions, inner thoughts, and memories.

75
00:05:05,750 --> 00:05:07,980
My feelings are tied to this piece of land; once it's gone, they will vanish too.

76
00:05:09,140 --> 00:05:15,700
I also remember that after class, my mom would often take me to eat snails nearby.

77
00:05:15,700 --> 00:05:21,680
Strangely, that snail stall has become very popular on TikTok recently.

78
00:05:21,680 --> 00:05:25,860
But when I was a child, it was just a local spot that only neighborhood residents knew about.

79
00:05:25,860 --> 00:05:30,440
Life back then had a strong sense of community.

80
00:05:30,440 --> 00:05:33,960
Even though it wasn't a traditional village, people in the same neighborhood looked out for each other.

81
00:05:34,900 --> 00:05:39,080
I still remember the days when I walked back from prep class feeling very sad.

82
00:05:39,080 --> 00:05:42,660
Even now, I still remember one specific day of class that absolutely terrified me.

83
00:05:43,420 --> 00:05:43,700
I had mistakenly used an eraser on ink.

84
00:05:44,320 --> 00:05:47,660
We had to transition from pencils to ink pens as we moved up from age five to first grade.

85
00:05:47,660 --> 00:05:53,280
But I accidentally used both an ink pen and an eraser, which was against the rules because it made the page messy.

86
00:05:53,280 --> 00:05:56,000
The teachers back then were very different from today.

87
00:05:56,710 --> 00:06:00,480
She always kept a very large wooden ruler on her desk,

88
00:06:00,480 --> 00:06:05,228
and she would strike any student who made a mistake. Sometimes, she hit so hard my hands would bruise.

89
00:06:05,228 --> 00:06:07,268
So I was absolutely terrified of using an eraser while writing with a fountain pen.

90
00:06:07,888 --> 00:06:11,868
I cried all the way home because of my mistake.

91
00:06:11,868 --> 00:06:16,728
To comfort me, my mother took me to the snail stall.

92
00:06:16,728 --> 00:06:20,248
And at that stall, the owner was playing the violin.

93
00:06:20,948 --> 00:06:24,008
That was the first time I ever learned what a violin was and how it was played.

94
00:06:24,808 --> 00:06:25,068
I grew up thinking that the violin could be played anywhere, even at a humble snail stall.

95
00:06:25,728 --> 00:06:28,308
It wasn't until I grew older that I realized violins are typically played in symphony orchestras.

96
00:06:28,928 --> 00:06:33,328
Those childhood feelings—my very first exposure to something new—shaped how I viewed that instrument ever since.

97
00:06:33,328 --> 00:06:38,768
To me, the violin has always felt very warm and accessible compared to other instruments, because I first heard it while eating snails.

98
00:06:38,768 --> 00:06:41,468
I don't know if this snail stall will be demolished in the future.

99
00:06:42,228 --> 00:06:48,108
But if it is, no other child will ever experience the violin for the first time in a snail stall again. [laughs]

100
00:06:48,108 --> 00:06:49,248
No one else will ever perceive the violin the way I did.

101
00:06:49,988 --> 00:06:51,368
I think this oral history is quite interesting.

102
00:06:52,448 --> 00:06:58,368
Because, as I said from the beginning, I didn't actually live in Trung Liet.

103
00:06:58,368 --> 00:07:02,128
It was just our store, so I didn't have many childhood memories associated with the area.

104
00:07:02,868 --> 00:07:06,628
I didn't play much there; I just went to class and went home. So Trung Liet rarely came to mind.

105
00:07:06,628 --> 00:07:10,700
However, when working on this project, I proposed talking about Trung Liet because it is about to be cleared.

106
00:07:11,668 --> 00:07:15,168
In my efforts to record and preserve these childhood memories of a place facing demolition,

107
00:07:15,168 --> 00:07:16,028
I found it to be a journey of self-discovery as well.

108
00:07:16,948 --> 00:07:18,408
Alongside recording the history, I rediscovered memories I had completely forgotten as I grew up.

109
00:07:19,468 --> 00:07:22,008
And I am very fortunate that before the area is cleared, I managed to recall them, filling a void in the pages of my childhood.

110
00:07:23,228 --> 00:07:28,508
Some of those memories are very precious.

111
00:07:28,508 --> 00:07:32,108
Imagine sitting in a concert hall, looking at the violin, and remembering you first heard it at a tiny boiled snail stall.

112
00:07:32,968 --> 00:07:34,508
Or commuting back from work and remembering the terror of a wooden ruler because you used an eraser with a fountain pen.

113
00:07:35,128 --> 00:07:37,508
It's a feeling [laughs] of realizing how much I've grown.

114
00:07:38,148 --> 00:07:39,868
Now I worry about other things, and I'm no longer afraid of an eraser.

115
00:07:40,608 --> 00:07:45,568
And finally, remembering the pure longing to just run free and play when I had to study.

116
00:07:45,568 --> 00:07:50,168
[laughs] It's very similar to the feeling of wanting to finish university assignments quickly just to go home and play games. [laughs]

117
00:07:50,168 --> 00:07:52,348
So, that was the childhood of a kid who once lived in Trung Liet.

118
00:07:53,348 --> 00:07:54,388
I hope that when this area is cleared, this project will keep those memories alive forever.

119
00:07:54,608 --> 00:07:58,808
[background music]
